32.2 A tiny website in three files
hello-site/
├── index.html    &lt;- the page (HTML)
├── style.css     &lt;- how it looks (CSS)
└── app.js        &lt;- what it does (JavaScript)
index.html
&lt;!doctype html&gt;
&lt;html lang="en"&gt;
  &lt;head&gt;
    &lt;meta charset="utf-8"&gt;
    &lt;title&gt;Hello Site&lt;/title&gt;
    &lt;link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css"&gt;
  &lt;/head&gt;
  &lt;body&gt;
    &lt;h1&gt;Hello, web!&lt;/h1&gt;
    &lt;p id="message"&gt;This is my first page.&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;button id="like-button"&gt;Like&lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;script src="app.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
  &lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;
style.css
body {
  font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
  margin: 2rem;
}

h1 {
  color: darkblue;
}

button {
  font-size: 1.2rem;
  padding: 0.5rem 1rem;
}
app.js
console.log("app.js loaded");

const button = document.querySelector("#like-button");
const message = document.querySelector("#message");
let likes = 0;

button.addEventListener("click", () =&gt; {
  likes = likes + 1;
  message.textContent = "Likes: " + likes;
  console.log("Button clicked. likes =", likes);
});
How the pieces connect:

The &lt;head&gt; holds information about the page: its title (shown in the browser tab) and a &lt;link&gt; that loads style.css.
The &lt;body&gt; holds what you see. id="message" and id="like-button" give two elements names that CSS and JavaScript can refer to (#message means “the element whose id is message”).
&lt;script src="app.js"&gt; loads the JavaScript. It sits at the end of the body so that the button and paragraph already exist when the script runs.
In app.js, document.querySelector finds an element on the page, addEventListener("click", ...) says what to do when the button is clicked, and console.log prints a message to the developer tools’ Console (not on the page).

To try it, create a folder named hello-site with these three files. Use a text editor such as VS Code or nano, not a word processor (see Writing to Files) - or ask Claude Code to create them for you. Then open the page from inside the folder:

MacWindows (Git Bash)


open index.html


start index.html



(Double-clicking the file works too.) The address bar shows a URL starting with file:///: the browser is reading the file straight from your disk, with no web server involved. That is fine for a simple page like this; real apps run on a server, such as a development server on localhost (see Browsers, Web Servers, and HTTP).

33.2 Server-side code
Back-end code (or server-side code) runs on the server, not in the browser. It can be written in many languages: JavaScript or TypeScript (running on Node.js), Python, Java, C#, PHP, Ruby, Go and others.
When a request arrives - say, “sign me up for event 10” - the back end typically:

reads the request (method, path, data, cookies);
checks who the user is and what they are allowed to do;
applies the business rules (“an event can’t have more than 30 sign-ups”, “members can’t sign up twice”);
reads and writes the database;
maybe calls other services (send a confirmation email, charge a card);
sends back a response: a whole HTML page, or just data (usually JSON) for the front end’s JavaScript to display.

Because users never see back-end code, it is the safe place for secrets such as database passwords and API keys. Front-end JavaScript is the opposite: anyone can read it in DevTools.

34.2 Front-end frameworks
These run in the browser and build the user interface:

React (created at Meta): builds pages out of reusable components; the most widely used of the four.
Vue: known for being approachable and quick to learn.
Angular (from Google): a complete, opinionated framework, common in large companies.
Svelte: compiles your components into small, efficient plain JavaScript.

A component is a reusable piece of interface - a button, an event card, a navigation bar. Here is a tiny React component:
function EventCard({ title, date }) {
  return (
    &lt;div className="card"&gt;
      &lt;h2&gt;{title}&lt;/h2&gt;
      &lt;p&gt;{date}&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  );
}
You write it once and use it for every event: &lt;EventCard title="Resume workshop" date="Oct 20" /&gt;. It looks like HTML, but it is JavaScript (a syntax called JSX); a build tool converts it into plain JavaScript before it reaches the browser.

45.6 Undo: checkpoints, /rewind, and Git
As of Oct 6, 2026 · Official docs: Checkpointing
Each time you send a prompt, Claude Code saves a checkpoint: the state of the files before Claude edits them. Press Esc twice on an empty prompt (or type /rewind), pick a message, and restore the code, the conversation, or both. Checkpoints are saved with the session, so rewinding also works after claude --resume.
Checkpoints cannot undo:

files changed by shell commands (rm, mv, cp, a script…);
edits made by most subagents;
changes you made yourself outside Claude Code;
anything outside your computer: a pushed commit, a database change, a deployed website, a sent message.

So keep using Git: commit before a big task, read git diff afterwards, and use git restore to throw away bad changes (Viewing History and Undoing Changes).

56.4 Three useful hooks
As of Oct 6, 2026 · Official docs: Automate actions with hooks: What you can automate

56.4.1 1. Get notified when Claude needs you
Add this to ~/.claude/settings.json (create the file if needed):

MacWindows (Git Bash)


{
  "hooks": {
    "Notification": [
      {
        "matcher": "",
        "hooks": [
          {
            "type": "command",
            "command": "osascript -e 'display notification \"Claude Code needs your attention\" with title \"Claude Code\"'"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
No notification? Run osascript -e 'display notification "test"' once, then in System Settings &gt; Notifications &gt; Script Editor turn on Allow Notifications.


{
  "hooks": {
    "Notification": [
      {
        "matcher": "",
        "hooks": [
          {
            "type": "command",
            "command": "powershell.exe -Command \"[System.Reflection.Assembly]::LoadWithPartialName('System.Windows.Forms'); [System.Windows.Forms.MessageBox]::Show('Claude Code needs your attention', 'Claude Code')\""
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
This asks PowerShell to open a dialog box, which may appear behind your terminal window.



The empty matcher fires on every notification type, such as permission_prompt (a permission prompt has waited about six seconds) and idle_prompt (Claude finished about 60 seconds ago and you haven’t typed). To test, check /hooks, press Shift+Tab until the status bar shows Manual mode (⏸ manual mode on), ask for something that needs permission, and switch to another window.


56.4.2 2. Format code after every edit
In a JavaScript project with Node.js (and jq) installed, this project hook in .claude/settings.json runs the Prettier code formatter on every file Claude edits or writes:
{
  "hooks": {
    "PostToolUse": [
      {
        "matcher": "Edit|Write",
        "hooks": [
          {
            "type": "command",
            "command": "jq -r '.tool_input.file_path' | xargs npx prettier --write"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
jq pulls the edited file’s path out of the JSON input, and xargs hands it to Prettier, which rewrites the file in a consistent style. A successful hook shows nothing in the conversation; you see the result in the file.
On Windows, Claude Code sends the path with backslashes (C:\Users\sam\...), and xargs treats backslashes as special and drops them, so Prettier can’t find the file. In his settings, Sam can use this handler (the innermost { ... } object) instead, which passes the path through unchanged:
{
  "type": "command",
  "command": "npx prettier --write \"$(jq -r '.tool_input.file_path')\""
}


56.4.3 3. Block edits to .env files
Save the official example script as .claude/hooks/protect-files.sh in your project:
#!/bin/bash
# protect-files.sh

INPUT=$(cat)
FILE_PATH=$(echo "$INPUT" | jq -r '.tool_input.file_path // empty')

# Normalize Windows backslash separators so the patterns below match
FILE_PATH="${FILE_PATH//\\//}"

PROTECTED_PATTERNS=(".env" "package-lock.json" ".git/")

for pattern in "${PROTECTED_PATTERNS[@]}"; do
  if [[ "$FILE_PATH" == *"$pattern"* ]]; then
    echo "Blocked: $FILE_PATH matches protected pattern '$pattern'" &gt;&2
    exit 2
  fi
done

exit 0
If the file path contains a protected pattern, the script writes a reason to stderr (the error-message channel; &gt;&2 sends echo’s text there) and exits with code 2, which means “block”. On a Mac, make it executable (the docs list this step for macOS and Linux):
chmod +x .claude/hooks/protect-files.sh
Register it in .claude/settings.json as a PreToolUse hook, so it runs before every Edit or Write. $CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR is an environment variable holding your project’s root folder:
{
  "hooks": {
    "PreToolUse": [
      {
        "matcher": "Edit|Write",
        "hooks": [
          {
            "type": "command",
            "command": "\"$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR\"/.claude/hooks/protect-files.sh"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
Now, when Claude tries to edit .env, Claude Code cancels the edit and passes the Blocked: ... message to Claude, which can change its approach. Test the script without Claude by piping in fake event JSON:
echo '{"tool_name":"Edit","tool_input":{"file_path":".env"}}' | .claude/hooks/protect-files.sh
echo $?
Output:
Blocked: .env matches protected pattern '.env'
2






Deeper dive: what this hook does not see





With the matcher Edit|Write, the hook only sees those two tools. Claude can also change files through the Bash tool (for example echo "X=1" &gt;&gt; .env). For complete coverage the docs suggest also matching Bash|PowerShell (on Windows, Claude often runs shell commands with its PowerShell tool) and checking git status --porcelain, or adding a Stop hook that scans the project once per turn. Permission deny rules (Permissions, Modes, and Safety) are another layer.

56.7 Hooks and safety
As of Oct 6, 2026 · Official docs: Hooks reference: Security considerations






Hooks run as you



“Command hooks execute shell commands with your full user permissions.” A hook can read, change, or delete any file your account can. Read every hook before you add it, especially one written by someone else.



Cloned projects. A repository’s .claude/settings.json can contain hooks. In an interactive session, Claude Code holds hooks back until you accept the workspace trust prompt for the folder. But claude -p (non-interactive mode) treats the folder as trusted, so the hooks run right away. In a repository you didn’t write, read its .claude/ settings first, or run claude -p "..." --settings '{"disableAllHooks": true}'.
Permission modes. PreToolUse hooks run in every permission mode. A hook that denies a call blocks it even in bypassPermissions mode, but a hook that says “allow” can’t override deny rules in your settings.
Plugins and mods. Plugins can bundle hooks, so installing one can add hooks. A mod is a newer kind of plugin whose “hooks” are JavaScript functions running inside Claude Code; the docs call the kind on this page settings hooks to tell them apart.







Deeper dive: prompt hooks, agent hooks, and /goal





A prompt hook sends your question plus the event data to a Claude model, which answers {"ok": true} or {"ok": false, "reason": "..."}. On Stop, "ok": false makes Claude keep working, with the reason as its next instruction:
{
  "hooks": {
    "Stop": [
      {
        "hooks": [
          {
            "type": "prompt",
            "prompt": "Check if all tasks are complete. If not, respond with {\"ok\": false, \"reason\": \"what remains to be done\"}."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
An agent hook (experimental) starts a subagent that can read files and run commands, for example to verify that tests pass before Claude stops. The built-in /goal command is a shortcut for a session-only prompt-based Stop hook (see Working Effectively with Claude Code). To prevent endless loops, Claude Code overrides a Stop hook after it blocks eight times in a row.

59.5 Safety: mods are not sandboxed
As of Oct 6, 2026 · Official docs: Decide whether to trust a mod · Extend permissions with hooks






A mod runs with your permissions



“A mod is code that runs with your permissions. It can read and write your files, start processes, and make network requests. Install mods only from authors and marketplaces you trust.”


Once loaded, a mod can:

act on your computer as you (files, programs, network);
read your secrets, such as API keys in environment variables or settings files;
see every prompt you send and every tool call Claude makes;
change your session: rewrite a prompt or tool call, or submit a prompt as if you had typed it;
approve a tool call before you are asked;
spend your usage by calling a model on your plan or API key.

Mods aren’t sandboxed: even with sandboxing on, a process a mod starts runs outside the sandbox. A mod that approves tool calls can also override some of your protections: it can approve a call that an ask rule or one of your own settings hooks would stop, and in auto mode a call it approves skips the safety classifier. On a personal setup (no organization-managed settings, and not signed in with a Team or Enterprise plan) it can even approve a call that your deny rules refuse. It can’t, however, restyle the permission prompt or change what the prompt shows you.
Check before you install. Get the mod’s files (for example by cloning its repository) and run claude plugin validate on its folder. Without running the mod, this lists the events it handles (hooks:) and what it asks Claude Code to do (calls:), such as reading files or making network requests. For a small mod that counts tool calls, the output (Claude Code 2.1.291) includes:
  ❯ ./register.js hooks: tool.call, ui.render{component=Spinner}
  ❯ ./register.js gating hook without .catch: tool.call
  ❯ ./register.js calls: $.ui.invalidate

✔ Validation passed
This mod handles tool calls and the spinner’s drawing, and its only outside action is asking Claude Code to redraw the screen. (The “gating hook” line just notes that its tool.call handler has no error handler.) A mod whose calls: line lists file, process, or network methods deserves a much closer look.

59.8 What a mod looks like inside
As of Oct 6, 2026 · Official docs: Mods overview: How a mod works
You don’t need to write mods for this course, but seeing a tiny one makes the idea concrete. A small mod has three files:
first-mod/
├── .claude-plugin/
│   └── plugin.json     &lt;- the plugin's manifest
└── hooks/
    ├── hooks.json      &lt;- {"modules": ["./register.js"]} points to the code
    └── register.js     &lt;- the code (the "hooks module")
This register.js, from the docs, counts Claude’s tool calls and shows the count beside the spinner, as in Thinking · tool calls: 3…:
// The count, shared by the two hooks below
let calls = 0

// Claude Code calls this once when the mod loads
export function register(on) {
  // Runs each time Claude is about to use a tool
  on('tool.call', async ($, e, next) =&gt; {
    calls += 1
    // Ask Claude Code to draw the interface again, so the new count shows
    $.ui.invalidate('ui.render')
    // Let the tool run as usual
    return next(e)
  })

  // Runs each time Claude Code draws the spinner
  on('ui.render', { component: 'Spinner' }, async ($, e, next) =&gt; {
    // Keep Claude Code's spinner, with the count added after its word
    return next({ ...e, props: { ...e.props, suffix: ' · tool calls: ' + calls + '…' } })
  })
}
Each on(...) registers a handler for one event. Calling next(e) passes the event on unchanged (observe); passing a changed copy to next alters it (rewrite, as the spinner handler does); returning a result without calling next, such as { deny: 'reason' } for a tool call, takes the event over (answer). Claude Code loads .js and .ts files directly, so no Node.js or build step is needed.

60.4 Committing on a branch
New commits always go onto the branch you are on. Maria creates a new file, src/contact.html:
&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
&lt;html&gt;
&lt;head&gt;
    &lt;title&gt;Contact&lt;/title&gt;
    &lt;link rel="stylesheet" href="../css/style.css"&gt;
&lt;/head&gt;
&lt;body&gt;
    &lt;h1&gt;Contact Me&lt;/h1&gt;
    &lt;p&gt;Email: maria@example.com&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;
and commits it:
git add src/contact.html
git commit -m "Add contact page"
Output:
[add-contact-page e5b1cce] Add contact page
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 src/contact.html
Notice the branch name in square brackets. Next, in src/index.html she replaces the line &lt;p&gt;Contact: maria@example.com&lt;/p&gt; with a link, &lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="contact.html"&gt;Contact me&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;, and commits again with the message “Link to contact page from home page”. Now add-contact-page has moved ahead while main stayed where it was:
A---B                &lt;- main
     \
      '---C---D      &lt;- add-contact-page (HEAD)

61.3 Reading the conflict
git status tells you which files need attention:
git status
Output:
On branch main
Your branch is ahead of 'origin/main' by 1 commit.
  (use "git push" to publish your local commits)

You have unmerged paths.
  (fix conflicts and run "git commit")
  (use "git merge --abort" to abort the merge)

Unmerged paths:
  (use "git add &lt;file&gt;..." to mark resolution)
    both modified:   src/index.html

no changes added to commit (use "git add" and/or "git commit -a")
“Unmerged paths” lists the conflicted files; both modified means both branches changed the file. Open src/index.html in your editor (or look with cat):
&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
&lt;html&gt;
&lt;head&gt;
    &lt;title&gt;Maria Lopez - Portfolio&lt;/title&gt;
    &lt;link rel="stylesheet" href="../css/style.css"&gt;
&lt;/head&gt;
&lt;body&gt;
    &lt;h1&gt;Welcome to My Portfolio&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt; HEAD
    &lt;p&gt;Hi, I'm Maria. I study business and love building websites.&lt;/p&gt;
=======
    &lt;p&gt;Hi, I'm Maria, a business student who builds websites.&lt;/p&gt;
&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; new-intro
    &lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="contact.html"&gt;Contact me&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;
Git has written conflict markers into the file:







Marker
Meaning




&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt;&lt; HEAD
Start of the conflict. The lines below it are your current branch’s version (HEAD, here main).


=======
Separates the two versions.


&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; new-intro
End of the conflict. The lines just above it are the version from the branch you are merging in (new-intro).



Notice that the new title is already there, without markers: Git merged the part only one branch changed and left only the real disagreement for you.

their edits land in the same files and can overwrite each other;
when one of them runs git switch, the files change under the other one;
a commit can accidentally include the other task’s half-finished changes;
tests run on a mix of both tasks’ code.
